  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthen Evidence-Based Migration Response through Enhanced Data Collection on Displaced Persons from Ukraine in Sweden</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Starting on 24 February 2022, in the full-scale invasion of Ukraine by the Russian Federation triggered an unprecedented humanitarian crisis across the country that resulted in mass internal displacement and large refugee flows to neighbouring countries and other countries in Europe. More than 8 million Ukrainians fled the country in less than a year. In addition, as of 23 January 2023, IOM estimates that 5.4 million are internally displaced across Ukraine, and more than 620,000 Third-Country Nationals (TCNs) have crossed the borders to neighbouring countries since the start of the war. 
 
To contribute to the improvement of migration management and service provision systems in Sweden, at the request of the Swedish Ministry of Justice, IOM proposes a rapid profiling survey amongst a sample of the Ukrainian refugees present in Sweden. The survey will collect data on the specific needs of this group, their past activities in Ukraine, and future perspective to support integration programming and host country policies. The survey will be anonymous and voluntary. As a result, national institutions and humanitarian responders will have access to accurate, up-to-date data and enhanced knowledge on refugees and TCNs arriving to and present in Sweden.</narrative>
  </description>
